<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christmas carol</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as noel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> carol is a song or hymn on the theme of Christmas, traditionally sung at Christmas or in the surrounding season. The first known Christmas hymns may be traced to 4th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and by the 13th century popular Christmas songs in vernacular languages had developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, under the influence of Francis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a>. The earliest English carols appear in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of 1426 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a> chaplain John Awdlay, who recorded twenty-five. Carols grew in popularity after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>, when reformers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> promoted congregational singing, and printed collections of the 19th century widened their appeal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christmas-carol.jpg" alt="Christmas carol" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prathyush Thomas · GFDL 1.2 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christmas_carol_group_BNC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The earliest English <b>carols</b> appear in a manuscript of 1426 by the Shropshire chaplain John Awdlay, who recorded twenty-five.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>The first known Christmas hymns may be traced to 4th-century Rome, and by the 13th century popular Christmas songs in vernacular languages had developed in France, Germany and particularly Italy; the earliest English examples of the <b>Christmas carol</b> appear in a manuscript of 1426.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>Although the plays were performed in summer, the lullaby is now sung as a <b>Christmas carol</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/coventry-carol">Coventry Carol</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Deck the Halls&quot; is a <b>Christmas carol</b> with a Welsh tune and English words.</blockquote><a href="/songs/deck-the-halls">Deck the Halls</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Es ist ein Ros entsprungen&quot; (&quot;A rose has sprung up&quot;) is a <b>Christmas carol</b> and Marian hymn of German origin, best known in English as &quot;Lo, How a Rose E'er Blooming&quot; and also called &quot;A Spotless Rose&quot; and &quot;Behold a Rose of Judah&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/es-ist-ein-ros-entsprungen">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Gaudete&quot; (&quot;Rejoice&quot;) is a sacred <b>Christmas carol</b> in Latin, thought to have been composed in the 16th century, though there is a known entry from around 1420 in the Hussite Jistebnice hymnal (Jistebnický kancionál).</blockquote><a href="/songs/gaudete">Gaudete</a></li></ul></section>
